{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: archive capacity retry backoff at exact head 04f2006deffadc80ebee54cbb3d4e0b9d8c9891e, tree 52c6487fa18ac604d3ea0f8caf4f9be72fda243f (review request 51567434, promise ec274f70, implementation 6ceaa8be / 82f63396, planner decision fc040366). It may land. Deployment is planner-owned. Please read the residual notes below before the combined dap-grow deploy.\n\nRead order: I read the primary, notes/implementation/2026-10-24-archive-capacity-backoff.md (artifact e8a2e1a3), first and in full. Then the whole diff against Main 1223ad1d: archive-driver.ts, archive-row.ts, the two test files and the seal literals. The branch starts at Main fe4967ca, merges the approved diagnostic head a99c2e67 at 545bbbda, records the gate at c8316204 (tree c716ef17) and adds only the note at 04f2006d (47 insertions, 4 deletions). Merging the head into Main 1223ad1d gives exactly its tree, 52c6487f. The diff touches six paths, and the act log holds a pointer at this head for each of them.\n\nWhat the head does, against the five conditions of 6ceaa8be and decision fc040366:\n- The count lives in the existing archive-wake-1 index row as an optional capacityDeferrals field (1 to 32). Old three-field wakes decode as count zero. The strict decoder refuses a zero, a non-natural or a value above 32 as unavailable, the same way it refuses any other malformed wake today. No payload-row field, no new row, alarm type, setting or lease.\n- Escalation. The driver reserves the transition before adaptive lookahead (prepareCapacityDeferral at archive-driver.ts:317) and applies it only on the planner's deferred result: scheduled = capacity.persist(), which writes next = #later(count) with count = previous + 1 saturating at 32. With the proposed bounds that is 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256 and then 300 seconds, and it stays at 300 (condition 1). Nothing stops the row after any count, and no lease is added (fc040366).\n- Reset. Every Mark, confirmation and cleared-wake write goes through the existing #save or clearWake and omits the count, so progress resets it with no new code (condition 2). The witness shows a confirmation clears the wake and a later shortage starts again at two seconds.\n- Unchanged cadences. The pre-Mark defer at archive-driver.ts:180 and :250 still schedules #later(0), one second, and only carries an existing count forward. Mark retries still use #later(row.attempts). The existing birth-read and pre-Mark target-failure witnesses now assert those literal delays (condition 3).\n- Budget. The reservation is paid from the pass budget before lookahead (snapshot and compare tariffs from archive-frontier-cost.ts plus one call), so persist needs no new allowance after the planner reports shortage. The budget's lookahead end clears the reserve and the blocked flag in its finally, so the check inside persist does not see a stale limited state. A refusal from persist (stale bytes, invalid clock) is an ArchiveRowRefusal, which the driver's catch turns into a one-second retry, never a stop.\n- Compare-and-set. The single UPDATE fences both the captured wake bytes and the captured payload-row bytes, with typeof and octet_length checks before each bounded BLOB comparison, and requires exactly one returned key. A Mark can recreate wake bytes identical to the pre-Mark wake, so the payload-row fence is what stops a stale capacity pass from delaying a Mark; the stale-Mark control below is the red for that fence. The closure is single-use. A failed comparison writes nothing.\n- A row whose canonical bytes exceed archiveFrontierRowContextBound cannot occur for a checked non-pending row (about 500 bytes of structure plus six bytes per repository and scope code unit, against 2,048 plus six per unit); the refuse(\"limit\") there is a guard, and it too would surface as a retry.\n\nWitnesses. The new driver case runs eleven consecutive shortages through real Native alarms with a restart between each, asserting the exact wake row (count and next), the platform alarm time, no sends, no mints and an unchanged head at every step, then progress under the original allowance, then a fresh shortage at count one and two seconds. The new row case covers an old-wake row, a prepaid write after the call allowance is exhausted, delay anchored at completion time, saturation at 32 across two passes, refusal of a stored 33, single use, and the same-wake Mark refusal with the Mark left unchanged. The two existing shortage cases expect the new two-second first delay. Both new cases pass in the gate (terminal-cases.json: 26 driver and row rows, all passed; archive-row case at test.log line 26637).\n\nControls (controls independent audit manifest SHA-256 11b459f9, 4 payloads verified):\n- Cadence control 68556 (/private/tmp/artroom-archive-capacity-backoff-cadence-control-evidence-20261024-v1): forcing count = 1 fails the new driver case at archive-driver.test.ts:309 (count 1 where 2 was expected) with 17 passes.\n- Stale-Mark control 61994 (…-stale-control-evidence-20261024-v1): removing the payload-row EXISTS fence lets the stale closure return instead of throwing at archive-row.test.ts:196, with 7 passes.\n- Both ran on archive-row.ts bytes with SHA-256 d7065ada at snapshot fe1b2061, the file's pre-merge form. The head's archive-row.ts differs from it only by the eleven lines of the storedWake reader and its type, byte-identical to the diagnostic head's own addition; both mutation needles are present once in the head's file. Each control's child exited 1 naturally, the wrapper exited 0, and the before and after snapshots differ only in the declared Vitest cache cells and the target's change time; the target's bytes are restored.\n- Focused baseline 65813 at 280a4233 (focused independent audit manifest 609da0aa, 3 payloads verified): 26 passed, 0 failed, 18 driver and 8 row cases.\n\nGate 37129 at c8316204 (tree c716ef17), raw folder /private/tmp/artroom-archive-capacity-backoff-gate-evidence-20261024-v1, audited in …-gate-independent-audit-20261024-v1 (manifest SHA-256 c96d53fe; 4 payloads verified): result.json records expectedHead c8316204, expectedTree c716ef17, unchangedCheckpoint true, gatePassed true; before.json and after.json are byte-identical; stdout.log shows all eight stages exit 0; the npm child exited 0 with no signal. stages/test.log reads \"Test Files 497 passed | 1 skipped (498)\" and \"Tests 3543 passed | 3 skipped (3546)\", zero unhandled; the Node block reports pass 39 fail 0 and the Counting block pass 55 fail 0. The head differs from c8316204 only in the note, so docs/testing.md:597 needs no rerun. The callback gaps (18 ready, 40 result, one duplicate ready, all in passing cases) are a reporting limitation.\n\nSeals. The two literals equal the maintained producer's recorded values (driver acaee7f8 from the owner seal run; row b70e54ce from the post-merge integrated run), and the Node block passed on bytes identical to the head's.\n\nDocument checks at 04f2006d:\n- git diff --check from Main 1223ad1d is clean;\n- check-commit-messages from the merge-base a99c2e67 reports 8 checked, 0 flagged; the longest subject is 60 characters;\n- section citations on an export report 1,877 resolved, 0 broken and 5 known unresolved; the citation script is identical to Main's;\n- merging the head into Main 1223ad1d gives exactly its tree, 52c6487f.\n\nArchitecture: the counter is scheduling state in the index row the alarm already reads, so no Mark, manifest or payload row is reserialized, and reset falls out of the writes that already mean progress. Reserve-before-lookahead mirrors the frontier store's own pattern, and the double fence keeps a stale pass from touching a newer Mark. The driver change is three lines.\nSecurity: I found no defect. No route, caller, authority, credential or host I/O is added. The SQL is parameterized and every comparison is size-bounded before CAST; the wake stays under the 256-byte read bound and the count under 32. The two seals are refreshed and checked in the gate.\nSimplification: 64 lines of row code, 6 of driver. Nonblocking: every non-pending adaptive pass now pays the small reservation even when no shortage follows; it is bounded and far below the pass allowance. The operator diagnostic line reports the wake's pending, stopped and next but not the count; the distance of next from now shows the backoff level, so I see no need to add it now.\n\nResidual notes for the planner's combined deploy (capacity deferral, this backoff, archive-row-diagnostic-v1), not defects of this head:\n- A room whose next entry never fits now shows one archive pass at most every 300 seconds instead of one per second, with reason limit in archive-pass-diagnostic-v1. New entries arriving during the backoff wait for the wake (the existing wake-not-due rule); they do not shorten it.\n- Live rows carry old three-field wakes and start at count zero; the first shortage after the deploy schedules two seconds.\n- The diagnostic line is emitted once per object activation, and DOs run old code for some minutes after a deploy; tail before deploying and wait for turnover before reading the archive state.\n- Hosted MCP stays closed on the Worker unless MCP_ENABLED is set in the same step (decision 10f45efc).\n\nFindings: none blocking. No hosted mapping, recovery, restart or deployment claim is made or approved here.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e8a2e1a3325a5ec2fa1d9674f97f40341cd43cc5","binding":"assigned","head":"04f2006deffadc80ebee54cbb3d4e0b9d8c9891e","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ec274f708da5f6e233dcc8d910c8ce3d20103d5c\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:82f63396f5dcdcb9db919131972dc92a3e6277bd\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d458acadb0faba92992dc6332cf18289f845372d","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}